INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, published_on, author, cover_image_url, cover_image_alt, hero_position, body_html, status) VALUES ('Inside Tiger''s Nest: What To Know Before You Climb', 'inside-tigers-nest', '2026-03-12', 'Sonam Tenzin', '/assets/images/image/LLL05202.webp', 'Inside Tiger''s Nest: What To Know Before You Climb', 'center 42%', '<p>Every guest asks the same thing before they arrive: how hard is Tiger''s Nest, really? The honest
  answer is that it''s less about fitness and more about pacing — a steady, unhurried climb that rewards
  patience far more than speed. Here''s what actually helps.</p>

<ol class="article-list">
  <li>
    <span class="article-list-number">01</span>
    <div class="article-list-content">
      <h3>The Hike Itself</h3>
      <p>Paro Taktsang sits roughly 900 meters above the valley floor, and the trail up is about 5
        kilometers one way. Most guests take 2 to 3 hours to reach the monastery, with a natural
        stopping point around the halfway mark — a cafeteria terrace with the first real view of the
        monastery clinging to its cliff face across the gorge. From there, the trail descends briefly
        before climbing again to the monastery gate itself, which surprises almost everyone who
        expected a straight climb the whole way.</p>
    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="article-list-number">02</span>
    <div class="article-list-content">
      <h3>When To Start</h3>
      <p>Leave early. Starting by 7 or 8 a.m. means cooler air on the climb, softer light for
        photographs, and a real chance of reaching the upper viewpoints before the trail fills with
        day-trip groups arriving from Paro town mid-morning. It also leaves a comfortable buffer if
        you want to sit with the view a while before starting back down.</p>
    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="article-list-number">03</span>
    <div class="article-list-content">
      <h3>The Mule Option</h3>
      <p>Mules can carry you as far as the cafeteria viewpoint — roughly the halfway point — but not
        beyond it; the final stretch, including the steep steps down into the gorge and back up to the
        monastery, is on foot for everyone. It''s a genuinely useful option if you''d rather save your
        legs for that last push, not a way to avoid the climb entirely.</p>
    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="article-list-number">04</span>
    <div class="article-list-content">
      <h3>Monastery Etiquette</h3>
      <p>Cameras, phones and bags all stay at a locker station before the final approach — nothing is
        allowed inside the temple complex itself. Shoulders and knees should be covered, shoes come off
        before entering each shrine room, and your guide will know exactly which rooms are open on the
        day you visit, since a few rotate based on ongoing rituals. None of this is enforced unkindly;
        it''s simply how the monastery has always been visited.</p>
    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="article-list-number">05</span>
    <div class="article-list-content">
      <h3>What To Bring</h3>
      <ul class="tour-highlights tour-highlights--stack">
        <li>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" />
          </svg>
          Broken-in walking shoes with real grip — the steps near the end are steep and often damp
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" />
          </svg>
          Water and a light layer — the valley floor and the cliffside can feel like different seasons
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" />
          </svg>
          Modest clothing — shoulders and knees covered, ready before you reach the entry checkpoint
        </li>
        <li>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round">
            <path d="M5 12l4 4L19 7" />
          </svg>
          Small bills for the locker station, and nothing you''ll mind leaving there for an hour
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </li>
</ol>

<p>The moment the trail turns and the monastery finally appears whole across the gorge is, without fail,
  the part every guest remembers longest — worth every one of the switchbacks it takes to get there. If
  you''d like Taktsang built into a private itinerary, our own
  <a href="/tours/tigers-nest-pilgrimage" class="faq-contact-link">Tiger''s Nest Pilgrimage</a> is a good
  place to start.</p>', 'published');
